summary:
The large perfect hash aggregate (up to 22 bits) now builds and is ready to judge. q13 runs 20–23% faster (644→504, 629→486, 646→521 ms over three bench runs). The full bench shows no real regression: q16, q18 and q19 looked worse in one run but were noise on re-run (+1.9%, -5.2%, -0.5%), and q19 has no GROUP BY at all.

Changes:
1) plan_aggregate.cpp, CanUsePerfectHashAggregate: if the total bits exceed perfect_ht_threshold, perfect hashing is still allowed up to max(threshold, 22) bits when all of these hold:
   - the threshold is not below its default of 12, so setting it lower still turns the large mode off;
   - the estimated input is at least slots/2;
   - slots × (sum of aligned state sizes + 1) × (threads + 1) ≤ max_memory/8;
   - the input subtree contains no DELIM_SCAN.
2) perfect_aggregate_hashtable: Combine is split into CombineRange(other, start, end) and TakeAllocator(other). CombineRange allocates from the source table's arena; TakeAllocator is called under the global lock. ScanRange scans a slot range using an addresses vector and arena supplied by the caller, so several threads can scan at once.
3) Lazy slot initialization: aggregate states are no longer initialized for all slots up front. A slot is initialized when AddChunk first sets it, or in Combine when the target slot is new. Destroy only visits set slots. Slots that are never used are never touched, so their memory is never paged in.
4) physical_perfecthash_aggregate: Combine goes stripe by stripe, with up to 64 disjoint slot stripes of at least 4096 slots, each with its own mutex. Each thread starts at a different stripe. The source is now parallel: an atomic cursor hands out blocks of 64K slots, and MaxThreads is slots/64K. Tables of 16 bits or fewer are a single block, so they keep the old single-threaded scan order.

The first version, without items 3 and the DELIM_SCAN guard, made q17 158% slower. Its correlated-subquery aggregate on partkey was estimated at 34.7M input rows but really got 59.7K, and each of 8 threads built a full 2M-slot table. Lazy initialization brought that down to +16% and made q13 faster as well. The DELIM_SCAN guard brought q17 back to noise (-4.1%).

Correctness: results matched perfect_ht_threshold=0 (hash aggregate) exactly, using EXCEPT ALL both ways on TEMP tables:
- 1.5M dense INT keys with NULL groups, using count, sum, min, avg with a FILTER clause, max/min on strings, and list;
- a two-column INT/SMALLINT group key;
- a 12-bit group, which goes through the old path but now with lazy init;
- empty input.
A q15-style aggregate (17-bit suppkey range over a date-filtered lineitem) took 84–98 ms against 116–141 ms with the large mode off.
